<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">As-Salih Ayyub</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1205">1205</a>–<a href="/years/1249">1249</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as al-Malik al-Salih Najm al-Din Ayyub, al-Salih Ayyub · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">As-Salih Ayyub was an Ayyubid prince who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasankeyf">Hisn Kayfa</a> and the Jazira before becoming sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1240. He held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1239 and again from 1245, retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> with Khwarazmian help in 1244 and built up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahri-mamluks">Bahri Mamluk</a> regiment. He died in 1249 while resisting Louis IX's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/as-salih-ayyub.jpg" alt="As-Salih Ayyub" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1555 artist, Bukhara · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malik_Salih_Ayyub_(detail),_Prince_of_Damascus_and_great-nephew_of_Saladin,_conversing_with_Dervishes_he_has_met_at_the_Mosque._(The_Pleasure_Garden,_by_Sadi_of_Shiraz._Bokhara,_1555._Bib._Nat.,_Paris.).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>As-Salih Ayyub was born on 5 November 1205, the son of the Ayyubid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-kamil">Al-Kamil</a> and a Nubian concubine, Ward Al-Muna, who served Al-Kamil's other wife Sawda bint Al-Faqih, the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-adil">Al-Adil</a>. In 1221 he became a hostage at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifth-crusade">Fifth Crusade</a>, while John of Brienne was held by Al-Kamil, until Damietta was rebuilt and restored to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. In 1232 he was given <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasankeyf">Hasankeyf</a> in the Jazira, which his father had taken from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artuqid-dynasty">Artuqids</a>. In 1234 his father sent him to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, which removed him from the succession in Egypt, after suspecting that he was conspiring with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-kamil">Al-Kamil</a> died in 1238, he left as-Salih his designated heir in the Jazira and another son, Al-Adil II, as heir in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. As-Salih took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1239 and used it as a base. His uncle Al-Salih Ismail captured Damascus from him in September 1239, and he was held prisoner at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-karak">Kerak</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shajar-al-durr">Shajar al-Durr</a>, who bore their son Khalil. In April 1240 An-Nasir released him and allied with him against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a>, and in June 1240 they entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> together, as Al-Adil was imprisoned by his own troops. As-Salih became the paramount ruler of the Ayyubid family.</p><p>He was insecure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, where the Ashrafiyya faction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> plotted to replace him with Ismail. He bought large numbers of Kipchak slaves, available in great numbers after the Mongol invasions, and formed two corps of up to 1,000 men each: the Bahriyya, garrisoned on Rawda Island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, and the smaller Jamdārīyah, who guarded him. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> later overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a>.</p><p>In 1244, at his invitation, the remnants of the Khwarezmian army, routed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. That year he defeated Ismail, allied with the crusader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>, at La Forbie. In 1245 he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mustasim">al-Musta'sim</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> awarded him the title of sultan. In 1246 he turned on the Khwarezmians and defeated them near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> led to a call for a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a> took the cross. In 1249 Louis invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seventh-crusade">Seventh Crusade</a> and occupied Damietta. As-Salih was fighting Ismail in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, returned quickly and encamped at al-Mansourah. He died on 22 November 1249 after his leg was amputated because of an abscess.</p><p>He had kept his heir, al-Muazzam <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turanshah">Turanshah</a>, at a distance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasankeyf">Hasankeyf</a>, and his widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shajar-al-durr">Shajar al-Durr</a> hid his death until Turanshah arrived. Turanshah's rule was brief and was followed by a long interregnum until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahri-mamluks">Bahri Mamluks</a> took power. As-Salih was the last major Ayyubid ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the last to combine rule of Egypt with effective rule of parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “As-Salih Ayyub” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They also relied more and more on mamluk regiments, above all <b>al-Salih Ayyub</b>'s Bahriyya, and so created a professional military establishment that proved more durable and cohesive than the dynasty it served.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Turanshah, the last Ayyubid sultan of Egypt, was assassinated by the mamluks of his late father, <b>al-Salih Ayyub</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Al-Salih Ayyub</b> took the city in 1245.</blockquote><a href="/state/ayyubid-emirate-of-damascus">Ayyubid Emirate of Damascus</a></li><li><blockquote>Al-Nasir Dawud was a poet and scholar who used Kerak as a base for intrigue, briefly occupying Jerusalem in 1239 and holding, then releasing and backing, the future sultan <b>al-Salih Ayyub</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ayyubid-emirate-of-kerak">Ayyubid Emirate of Kerak</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1232 the Ayyubid sultan al-Kamil took Hisn Kayfa and Amid from the Artuqids of Hasankeyf and gave them to his son <b>al-Salih Ayyub</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ayyubids-of-hisn-kayfa">Ayyubids of Hisn Kayfa</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in Mansoura, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1233" aria-label="Cairo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5361 1067 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of As-Salih Ayyub"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5811 1337h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5811 1337h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1233">What was here in 1233? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
